      "problem": "Find the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4.$",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\text{d}x+4\\int {x}^{0}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+4(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1})+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1/2}+4x+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
